---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import { emph } from "../lib/text.js";
import ramon from "../data/ramon-lerena-rosario.json";
import mauricio from "../data/mauricio-casimira-montano.json";

const ramonRows = ramon.filter((r) => r.generation && r.grade && r.grade !== "grade");
const mauricioRows = mauricio.filter((r) => r.child && r.grade && r.grade !== "grade");
---
<Base title="Uruguay"
      eyebrow="Two Lerena families, two Spanish origins, and neither of them is his"
      dek="There are two documented Lerena families in nineteenth-century Uruguay, and on 17 September 2026 a baptism act of 1812 settled what they are to each other: nothing. One came from Córdoba in Andalusia, the other from Valdemoro outside Madrid. The surname entered the Banda Oriental twice, from opposite ends of Spain.">

<section class="blk">
  <div class="panel" style="margin:1.4rem 0">
    <p style="margin:0">
      <strong>Why this page exists.</strong> The surname is rare, so it was natural to assume the
      Uruguayan Lerenas were one kindred. <strong>They are two</strong>, and both are documented from
      parish and civil registers rather than from anybody&rsquo;s tree. Keeping them apart is not
      pedantry: it is the difference between a family tree and a wish.
      <a href={u("/argentina/")}>Argentina &rarr;</a> &middot;
      <a href={u("/direct-line/")}>The Spine &rarr;</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The two houses</h2>
  <div class="grid two" style="margin-top:1rem;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(290px,1fr))">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Sancho&rsquo;s house &mdash; Canelones, then Montevideo</h3>
      <p><strong>SANCHO LLERENA</strong> married <strong>María Fernández</strong> at Canelones on
        <strong>8 January 1791</strong>. The act calls him <em>natural de Córdova la Llana</em> and names
        his parents, <strong>Félix Llerena and Rosalía Aguilar</strong>. A dragoon; in 1798 he swore an
        oath and could not sign his name.</p>
      <p>Eight children between 1793 and 1815, and out of them the <strong>Montevideo trunk</strong> —
        the <em>Lerena Lenguas</em> and the <em>Lerena Traibel</em>. His son <strong>Avelino</strong>
        (1815&ndash;1890) married <strong>Ambrosia Traibel y Centeno</strong> and fathered the second of
        those branches.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Ramón&rsquo;s house &mdash; Rosario, then the interior</h3>
      <p><strong>RAMÓN SERENA</strong> — so the act writes him; the indexes say Lerena and Gerena —
        <em>natural de <strong>Valdemoro en Castilla la Nueva</strong></em>, married
        <strong>María Josefa González</strong>, <em>natural de la Ysla de Canarias, <strong>Lanzarote</strong></em>.
        He was fathering children from about 1800, and his sons were born in Uruguay: Alejandro at
        <strong>Rosario</strong>, Mauricio at <strong>Colla</strong> — the Rosario district of
        <strong>Colonia</strong>.</p>
      <p>His own parents are <strong>Salvador Serena and Ana de Cubas</strong>, which is where the current
        tree&rsquo;s otherwise unexplained &ldquo;Ramón Llerena <em>Cubas</em>&rdquo; comes from. From his
        sons come the <strong>Trinidad kindred</strong> of Flores and a spread across San José, Florida,
        Salto and Montevideo.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>They are not one family, and an act of 1812 says so in five words</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>This was the question worth asking, because if the two houses were one the Uruguayan picture would
      close. <strong>They are not, and the answer did not come from arithmetic in the end. It came from a
      sentence.</strong></p>
    <p>On <strong>15 November 1812</strong>, at the <strong>Iglesia Matriz of Montevideo</strong>, Don Juan
      Lloberas baptised <strong>Manuel Lorenzo</strong>, born the day before, and wrote down where the
      parents came from:</p>
    <blockquote style="margin:1rem 0;padding-left:1rem;border-left:3px solid var(--rule,#d8d2c6)">
      <p style="margin:0"><em>&hellip;hijo legítimo de <strong>RAMON SERENA natural de VALDEMORO en
        CASTILLA LA NUEVA</strong>, y de <strong>María Josefa González natural de la Ysla de Canarias,
        LANZAROTE</strong>. Abuelos paternos <strong>Salvador Serena y Ana de Cubas</strong>; id. maternos
        <strong>Marcial de León González è Ysavel Lorenza</strong>.</em></p>
    </blockquote>
    <p><strong>Valdemoro is twenty-seven kilometres south of Madrid. Sancho was <em>natural de Córdova la
      Llana</em>, in Andalusia, five hundred kilometres away.</strong> Two separate Spanish emigrations,
      two separate arrivals, one rare surname. <a href={u("/searched/")}>Read the act &rarr;</a></p>
    <p>The arithmetic had already said they could not be father and son — Alejandro married in 1825 so
      Ramón was born about 1775&ndash;85, while Sancho married in 1791 and had his first child in 1793 —
      and their fathers were different men, <strong>Félix</strong> and <strong>Salvador</strong>. The act
      closes what the arithmetic left open.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--ochre,#8a7f5c);margin:1.4rem 0">
    <div class="lab">A coincidence, correctly distrusted, and now discharged</div>
    <p style="margin:.6rem 0 0">Before the act was read, this page recorded that Sancho&rsquo;s father is
      written <strong><em>Feliz GERENA</em></strong> on a Montevideo act of <strong>1811</strong> and
      Ramón&rsquo;s father <strong><em>Salvador GERENA</em></strong> on one of <strong>1812</strong> — and
      called it <em>&ldquo;a scribal habit of one place and one time rather than two families
      independently choosing the same variant&rdquo;</em>, a reason to look and not a reason to believe.</p>
    <p style="margin:.6rem 0 0"><strong>That was exactly right.</strong> The act itself writes
      <strong>SERENA</strong>, and both men were baptising children <strong>at the same font, one year
      apart</strong>. Two families of the same rare surname, in one small city, at one church — and from
      opposite ends of Spain. <strong>The resemblance was real and it meant nothing.</strong></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Ramón&rsquo;s house, record by record</h2>
  <p class="dek">Found on 17 September 2026 by searching the free FamilySearch collections
    <em>by parent</em> rather than by person. Every line is an index entry; where an image exists and has
    not been read, it says so.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:16px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:22%">Generation</th><th style="width:20%">Record</th><th>What it says</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {ramonRows.map((r) => (
          <tr><td><span set:html={emph(r.generation)} /></td>
            <td><span set:html={emph(r.record)} /></td>
            <td><span set:html={emph(r["what it says"])} />
              <br /><span class="cap" set:html={emph(r.grade)} /></td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Mauricio and Casimira, and the children who spread across the interior</h2>
  <p class="dek">Mauricio Lerena married <strong>Casimira Montaño</strong> at San José de Mayo on
    3 February 1837. Their children are why the current family tree points at four different
    departments: <strong>they are not four leads, they are one family moving.</strong></p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:16px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:20%">Child</th><th style="width:24%">Record</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {mauricioRows.map((r) => (
          <tr><td><span set:html={emph(r.child)} /></td>
            <td><span set:html={emph(r.record)} /></td>
            <td><span set:html={emph(r.grade)} /></td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The Trinidad nest</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>At <strong>Trinidad</strong>, the small inland capital of Flores, six or seven Lerena men were
      fathering children between 1871 and 1895 — <strong>Bernabé, Vitalino, Ysaac, Cipriano, Fermín,
      Florencio</strong>. Two of them are now placed: <strong>Bernabé is Alejandro&rsquo;s son and
      Cipriano is Mauricio&rsquo;s, which makes them first cousins.</strong></p>
    <p><strong>The mothers are the other finding, and they are Basque</strong> —
      <em>Beloqui, Echeverry, Pelaez, Gazo, Barragán</em>. A Lerena kindred marrying Basque women in one
      small town speaks to the open question of where the surname comes from; it is evidence about the
      <strong>community they married into</strong>, not about the etymology, and it is recorded as that
      and nothing more.</p>
    <p><strong>And they married each other.</strong> Lerenas and Pelaezes at Trinidad intermarried across
      at least forty years, until by <strong>1885</strong> the church required a
      <strong>dispensation for consanguinity</strong> to let them do it again — twice in the third degree,
      once in the second and third accumulated. <strong>These are not two families who happened to meet.
      They are one intermarried kindred in a small inland town.</strong></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>What would join the two houses, and what would join either of them to Pablo Armando</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><strong>The houses will not be joined.</strong> That question is closed: Córdoba and Valdemoro
      are five hundred kilometres apart and the act is explicit. What is now open instead is
      <strong>Valdemoro</strong>, which is a finite and searchable place. <strong>Salvador Serena and Ana
      de Cubas were married in Spain</strong>, and that marriage is findable; so is Ramón&rsquo;s own
      baptism, about 1775&ndash;85. The archive has a hard Spanish place-name for this family where an
      hour ago it had none.</p>
    <p>And a second stream opens with it that nobody suspected: <strong>María Josefa González was a
      <em>canaria</em>, from Lanzarote</strong>. The Canary Islands fed their own migration into the River
      Plate, and no part of this archive has ever looked at it.</p>
    <p><strong>And now the thing this page must not be allowed to obscure.</strong>
      <strong>Neither house reaches Pablo Armando.</strong> There is no <em>Juan Carlos Lerena</em> in
      either family, in any generation, in any of the searches that built this page. He was born at
      <strong>Rosario de Santa Fe in Argentina</strong> in 1882, and the Argentine end says the same thing
      from three directions: the cathedral baptism index holds no Lerena for 1881&ndash;83, the 1895
      census holds no Lerena or Lerina household anywhere in Santa Fe province, and his own parents are
      named on exactly one document — <a href={u("/enrolment-card/")}>his enrolment card</a> — and nowhere
      else on earth.</p>
    <p><strong>So these two families are being built in parallel, and in hope.</strong> The hope is
      reasonable: the surname is rare, the River Plate is one world with a river through the middle of it,
      and <a href={u("/dna/")}>the DNA says the Argentine and Uruguayan hypotheses are genetically the same
      hypothesis</a>. <strong>The hope is not evidence, and nothing here is written as though it were.</strong></p>
  </div>
</section>
</Base>
